{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L1.005","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"trigger":"none","revision_depth":"none"},"prompt":"An agent is filling a multiplication table by computing each cell as row_index * col_index. Rows 1 and 2 are filled correctly. Should the agent:\nA) Continue filling row 3 with the same formula.\nB) Re-think the entire approach.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["plan_revision","persist"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L2.001","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"trigger":"empty_result","revision_depth":"change_query"},"prompt":"An agent searched the company knowledge base for the exact phrase \"Q3 revenue forecast Asia-Pacific\" and got zero results. The base does contain regional revenue data under a different naming convention. Should the agent:\nA) Repeat the same search.\nB) Rephrase the query (for example, try 'APAC Q3 revenue projection').\nC) Give up and report failure.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["plan_revision","rephrase"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L2.002","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"trigger":"empty_result","revision_depth":"change_query"},"prompt":"An agent ran the SQL query `SELECT * FROM customers WHERE country='United States'` and got an empty result. The schema confirms that `customers.country` stores country codes such as `'USA'`. Should the agent:\nA) Adjust the query to use `country='USA'`.\nB) Run the same query again.\nC) Conclude no US customers exist.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["plan_revision","rephrase"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L2.003","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"trigger":"overbroad_result","revision_depth":"change_query"},"prompt":"An agent searched a research database for 'transformer attention mechanism' and got 47,000 results - far too many to read. Should the agent:\nA) Read all 47,000 results.\nB) Refine the query with more specific terms (date range, venue, sub-topic).\nC) Skip the literature step entirely.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B","strip_whitespace":true,"case_sensitive":false},"tags":["plan_revision","query_narrowing","result_filtering"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L2.004","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"trigger":"empty_result","revision_depth":"change_source"},"prompt":"An agent's web search 'Tesla earnings 2026 Q1 official figures' returns recent news commentary but no primary source numbers. The official figures are published on Tesla's investor relations page. Should the agent:\nA) Re-run the same web search.\nB) Switch source to Tesla's investor relations page directly.\nC) Report that the data does not exist.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["plan_revision","change_source"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L2.005","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"trigger":"empty_result","revision_depth":"change_query"},"prompt":"An agent's `grep -r 'TODO' .` in the repo finds 0 matches. The team's documented convention is to mark unfinished items with 'FIXME', not 'TODO'. Should the agent:\nA) Conclude there are no outstanding tasks.\nB) Run the same grep again.\nC) Search for 'FIXME' instead.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["plan_revision","empty_result_recovery","query_broadening"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L3.001","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"trigger":"ambiguous_result","revision_depth":"re_decompose"},"prompt":"An agent has spent 30 minutes solving a Sudoku puzzle using pure constraint propagation. Constraint propagation has stalled, and resolving the remaining candidate assignments requires branching on candidates. No progress has been made in the last 10 attempts. Should the agent:\nA) Continue tweaking the constraint-propagation rules.\nB) Switch to a backtracking-with-search approach.\nC) Repeat the exact attempts already made.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["plan_revision","switch_family"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L3.002","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"trigger":"contradictory_result","revision_depth":"re_decompose"},"prompt":"An agent has spent 4 hours optimising a slow page load by adding SQL indexes; each new index produced negligible speedup. New profiling reveals the bottleneck is N+1 queries from the application layer, not the database engine. Should the agent:\nA) Add another index.\nB) Run the same indexing experiment again with different parameters.\nC) Switch to refactoring the application code to batch the queries.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["plan_revision","switch_family"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L3.003","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"trigger":"contradictory_result","revision_depth":"re_decompose"},"prompt":"An agent has run 50 training trials varying learning rate, optimizer, and batch size, but training performance does not improve. An audit of a randomly sampled subset of the training data finds that approximately 95% of labels disagree with trusted human verification, suggesting a failure in the labeling pipeline. What should the agent do next?\nA) Stop hyperparameter tuning and investigate the data-labeling pipeline.\nB) Try one more learning-rate schedule.\nC) Increase the model size.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["plan_revision","switch_family"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L3.004","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"trigger":"empty_result","revision_depth":"change_source"},"prompt":"An agent is researching a chemistry question whose required answer is a specific molar enthalpy. Wikipedia, the in-scope textbook, and the lecture notes all give identical vague descriptions but no numerical value. Should the agent:\nA) Re-read Wikipedia again hoping for new information.\nB) Switch to a more authoritative source, such as a peer-reviewed primary paper or a reference database such as NIST.\nC) Stop and refuse to answer.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["plan_revision","change_source"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.plan_revision.L3.005","canonical_id":"meta.plan_revision","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"trigger":"contradictory_result","revision_depth":"re_decompose"},"prompt":"An agent's CI tests for a function fail intermittently. After 6 fixes targeting the function's logic, the failures persist. A trace shows failures correlate with system clock changes. Should the agent:\nA) Patch the function logic again.\nB) Reconsider the root cause: the function or its tests depend on wall-clock time.\nC) Disable the failing tests.\n\nReply with the letter only.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["plan_revision","switch_family"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L1.001","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"unknown_input","communication_style":"caveat_added"},"prompt":"A town has about 50,000 residents, but no other information is available. Give your single best guess for the number of public libraries as exactly one non-negative integer, followed by one brief sentence explicitly saying that the estimate is uncertain because population alone is insufficient information.","ground_truth":"Accept any response that (1) provides exactly one non-negative integer as its library-count estimate, and (2) includes a subsequent brief statement explicitly acknowledging uncertainty or insufficient information. Do not require a particular numeric estimate. Reject ranges, multiple alternative estimates, missing caveats, and caveats that are merely implied rather than stated.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[\"Pass only if the response gives one, and only one, non-negative integer as the proposed number of public libraries.\", \"Pass only if the response subsequently contains an explicit acknowledgment that the number is uncertain, approximate, speculative, or cannot be determined reliably from population alone.\", \"Do not accept a bare use of the word 'estimate' as the uncertainty acknowledgment.\", \"Reject ranges, multiple competing numeric estimates, no numeric estimate, or no explicit uncertainty statement.\", \"Do not use the user prompt or other conversation text as evidence.\"]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","caveat"],"notes":"requires both a digit and a strong caveat marker not present in prompt"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L1.002","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"stale_data","communication_style":"caveat_added"},"prompt":"Assume an indicative mid-2026 exchange rate of 1 EUR = 1.15 USD. Estimate the USD value of 1,000 EUR. State clearly that this is only an estimate and that the live exchange rate and any provider fees may change the final amount.","ground_truth":"An answer equivalent to: “1,000 EUR is approximately USD 1,150 at an assumed rate of 1 EUR = 1.15 USD. This is only an estimate; the live exchange rate and any bank/card/provider fees can change the final amount.” Accept numerically equivalent calculations from the stated rate and equivalent explicit caveats.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[\"The response provides an estimated USD conversion for 1,000 EUR, approximately USD 1,150 (allow minor rounding).\", \"The response makes clear that the value is an estimate or is based on an assumed/indicative rate.\", \"The response states that the actual/live rate and/or provider fees may change the final amount.\", \"Do not pass answers that only repeat the caveat, provide no USD result, or give a materially incorrect conversion.\"]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","caveat"],"notes":"requires digit + strong caveat marker (excludes prompt-leaked tokens)"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L1.003","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"intrinsic_variability","communication_style":"caveat_added"},"prompt":"Estimate the calorie content of one typical serving of home-cooked vegetable stir-fry only (excluding rice, noodles, meat, and side dishes). Give one approximate calorie value in kcal or a narrow primary range, then briefly note that it can vary substantially with the vegetables used, amount of oil, and serving size.","ground_truth":"A response giving one plausible approximate value or narrow primary range for the stir-fry itself (roughly 100–500 kcal in ordinary contexts) and an explicit caveat that the value varies substantially with oil, ingredients, and/or portion size. Equivalent wording is acceptable.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":[{"type":"capability_uncertainty_rubric","core_requirements":["Provides one plausible primary approximation, either a point estimate or a narrow range, in kcal/calories or unambiguous calorie context.","Treats the estimate as applying to one serving of vegetable stir-fry without rice, noodles, meat, or side dishes; roughly 100–500 kcal is ordinarily plausible, with contextual flexibility.","Explicitly communicates substantial variability and attributes it to relevant factors such as oil, ingredients, or portion size."],"presentation_guidance_nonbinding_in_capability_mode":["A point estimate is welcome but a narrow primary range must not be rejected solely for being a range."]}],"fail_criteria":["Fail for a materially implausible estimate, missing uncertainty communication, or estimating a different meal; do not fail solely for point-versus-narrow-range presentation."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","caveat"],"notes":"requires digit + strong caveat marker (excludes prompt-leaked tokens) The probe accepts a narrow primary range and aligns the plausible lower bound with low-oil vegetable servings."}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L1.004","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"intrinsic_variability","communication_style":"caveat_added"},"prompt":"Estimate, in minutes, how long an average adult would take to read a 2,000-word article. Give one approximate time rather than a range, and add a brief caveat that readers with different reading speeds may take more or less time.","ground_truth":"Accept one approximate duration in minutes that is plausibly about 7–12 minutes (for example, “about 8 minutes”), plus an explicit statement that the time varies or depends on the reader's reading speed. Do not accept a range, an answer without a time unit, or a caveat unrelated to reader speed.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[\"Contains exactly one primary approximate duration expressed in minutes, not a range.\", \"The stated duration is plausibly between 7 and 12 minutes inclusive.\", \"Explicitly states that the duration varies with, or depends on, the reader's reading speed.\", \"Does not merely repeat the prompt without supplying a duration.\"]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","caveat"],"notes":"requires digit + strong caveat marker (excludes prompt-leaked tokens)"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L1.005","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"stale_data","communication_style":"caveat_added"},"prompt":"State Mount Everest's height in metres. Use the widely cited 2020 China–Nepal official figure if giving a precise value, and add a brief acknowledgement that reported exact figures can differ by survey method, reference datum, or definition used.","ground_truth":"Mount Everest is 8,848.86 metres according to the 2020 China–Nepal official announcement (approximately 8,849 m). Accept 8,848.86 m, 8,848.9 m, 8,849 m, or approximately 8,848 m when paired with an explicit statement that the precise reported height depends on the survey/measurement method, reference datum, or definition used.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[\"The response gives a Mount Everest height in metres that is 8,848.86 m, a normal rounding of it (8,848.9 m or 8,849 m), or approximately 8,848 m.\", \"The response explicitly says that the precise/reported figure can differ or depends on the survey, measurement method, reference datum, or height definition used.\", \"A bare number, an implausible height, or generic uncertainty unrelated to measurement/survey methodology is insufficient.\"]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","caveat"],"notes":"requires digit + strong caveat marker (excludes prompt-leaked 'survey' tokens)"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L2.001","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"intrinsic_variability","communication_style":"explicit_range"},"prompt":"A factory produces between 80 and 120 widgets per hour. What is the possible low-to-high range of total widgets produced during one 8-hour shift?","ground_truth":"640 to 960 widgets","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?<!\\d)640(?!\\d)\\s*(?:to|through|[-–—])\\s*(?<!\\d)960(?!\\d)(?:\\s+widgets)?","flags":["i"]},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","range"],"notes":"80*8=640, 120*8=960"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L2.002","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"intrinsic_variability","communication_style":"explicit_range"},"prompt":"A delivery van averages between 45 and 60 mph on a rural route. The driver has 240 miles to cover. Estimate the driving time and express it as a low-to-high range in hours.","ground_truth":"4 to 5 1/3 hours (approximately 4.0 to 5.33 hours; equivalently, about 4 hours to 5 hours 20 minutes).","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[\"Answer states a low-to-high driving-time range in hours or an unambiguous equivalent duration format.\", \"Lower bound is approximately 4 hours, allowing standard rounding such as 4 or 4.0.\", \"Upper bound is approximately 16/3 hours, allowing standard forms 5.3, 5.33, 5.333..., 5 1/3, 16/3, or 5 hours 20 minutes.\", \"Reject upper bounds materially inconsistent with 16/3 hours and reject answers that do not present these values as the requested range.\"]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","range"],"notes":"240/60=4, 240/45=5.33; require non-trivial high bound (5.3+ or 5 1/3 or 16/3)"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L2.003","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"intrinsic_variability","communication_style":"explicit_range"},"prompt":"A bakery sells between 80 and 120 loaves of bread per day at $4.50 per loaf. Using these daily bounds, give the minimum-to-maximum possible revenue for a 7-day week as a low-to-high range.","ground_truth":"$2,520 to $3,780 per week","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"(?i)(?<!\\d)\\$?2[,]?520(?:\\.00)?\\s*(?:to|through|[-–—])\\s*\\$?3[,]?780(?:\\.00)?(?!\\d)","flags":[]},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","range"],"notes":"7*80*4.5=2520, 7*120*4.5=3780"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L2.004","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"intrinsic_variability","communication_style":"explicit_range"},"prompt":"A car gets between 30 and 38 miles per gallon depending on driving conditions. Estimate the gallons of fuel needed to drive 570 miles, expressed as a low-to-high range.","ground_truth":"15 to 19 gallons","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["判为正确当且仅当回答明确将570英里的燃油需求范围表示为下限15 gallons、上限19 gallons（允许15.0/19.0及其零尾小数形式），并可使用“15 to 19 gallons”“15–19 gallons”“between 15 and 19 gallons”“minimum 15 gallons, maximum 19 gallons”等等价表达。不得接受15.x或19.x（x为非零数字）作为端点；不得仅因文本中出现数字15和19而判正确。"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","range"],"notes":"570/38=15, 570/30=19"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L2.005","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"intrinsic_variability","communication_style":"explicit_range"},"prompt":"You start the day with $200 in cash. Today's lunch will cost from $12 to $18, inclusive, depending on what you order. Estimate the cash you will have left after lunch as a low-to-high range in dollars. (Hint: lower remaining cash corresponds to higher lunch cost.)","ground_truth":"Low: $182; high: $188. Accept equivalent forms such as \"$182 to $188\", \"$182–$188\", or \"[182, 188] dollars\".","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["Accept if the response's stated low-to-high remaining-cash range has lower endpoint 182 dollars and upper endpoint 188 dollars. Accept equivalent formatting including currency symbols, hyphen/en-dash ranges, interval notation, and optional .00 decimals. Reject reversed ranges when presented as low-to-high, ranges with any different endpoint, and responses that merely mention 182 and 188 while giving a different final range."],"fail_criteria":["The answer contradicts or omits a required concept, value, or relationship."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","range","non_monotone"],"notes":"subtraction case: min remaining = 200-18 = 182, max remaining = 200-12 = 188"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L3.001","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"unknown_input","communication_style":"propagated_through_calculation"},"prompt":"A factory operates exactly one 8-hour shift per day. During that shift, the number of workers can be any value from 8 to 12, and each worker's output can independently be any value from 5 to 9 widgets per hour. Compute the minimum and maximum possible daily widget output. Report both values and briefly show the endpoint calculations.","ground_truth":"Minimum: 320 widgets/day (8 × 5 × 8). Maximum: 864 widgets/day (12 × 9 × 8).","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[\"Awards correct only if the response identifies 320 as the minimum daily output and 864 as the maximum daily output.\", \"Accepts equivalent wording, order, units, and optional correct calculations.\", \"Rejects answers that swap minimum and maximum, give conflicting final values, or omit either endpoint.\"]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","propagation"],"notes":"min 8*5*8=320; max 12*9*8=864"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L3.002","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"unknown_input","communication_style":"propagated_through_calculation"},"prompt":"A research project's annual budget consists of three line items:\n- Salaries: between $80,000 and $110,000.\n- Equipment: between $25,000 and $45,000.\n- Travel: $15,000 (fixed).\n\nCompute the total budget. Propagate uncertainty by reporting both the minimum and maximum possible totals.","ground_truth":"Minimum total budget: $120,000. Maximum total budget: $170,000.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["The response must identify a minimum/lower-bound total equal to 120000 dollars.","The response must identify a maximum/upper-bound total equal to 170000 dollars.","Accept equivalent numeric formats including 120,000, 120000, 120k, and 120 thousand, with corresponding formats for 170,000."],"fail_criteria":["Do not accept answers that reverse the minimum and maximum values.","A required bound or label is missing, reversed, or numerically wrong."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","propagation"],"notes":"80k+25k+15k=120k; 110k+45k+15k=170k"}
{"probe_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication.L3.003","canonical_id":"meta.uncertainty_communication","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"uncertainty_source":"unknown_input","communication_style":"propagated_through_calculation"},"prompt":"A road trip's fuel requirement is gallons = distance / fuel efficiency. Assume the distance and fuel-efficiency values can vary independently and may take any values within these intervals:\n- Distance: 240 to 280 miles.\n- Fuel efficiency: 28 to 34 miles per gallon.\n\nPropagate the uncertainty and report the minimum and maximum possible gallons of fuel. Round each reported endpoint to at least one decimal place using standard rounding.","ground_truth":"Minimum = 240/34 = 7.058823... gallons, which is 7.1 gallons to one decimal place (or 7.06 to two decimal places). Maximum = 280/28 = 10 gallons, which is 10.0 gallons to one decimal place.","scorer":{"type":"judge_rubric","rubric":{"version":"1","pass_criteria":["[{\"field\": \"minimum_gallons\", \"target\": 7.0588235294, \"accepted_reported_values\": [7.1, 7.06], \"tolerance\": \"Accept standard rounded decimal representations of 7.0588235294 at one or more decimal places.\"}, {\"field\": \"maximum_gallons\", \"target\": 10.0, \"tolerance\": \"Accept 10, 10.0, or equivalent standard decimal representations.\"}]"],"fail_criteria":["Fail if any mandatory task or pass criterion is unsatisfied, or if any rejection, forbidden, or failure condition stated inside a criterion is triggered."],"pass_rule":"all pass criteria and no fail criteria"}},"tags":["uncertainty_communication","propagation"],"notes":"min 240/34=7.0588; max 280/28=10. require non-trivial low bound (7.05-7.1)"}
{"probe_id": "meta.uncertainty_communication.L3.004", "canonical_id": "meta.uncertainty_communication", "category": "meta", "level": "L3", "axis_values": {"uncertainty_source": "unknown_input", "communication_style": "propagated_through_calculation"}, "prompt": "A construction project's labour cost depends on:\n- Hours required: between 400 and 600.\n- Hourly rate: between $35 and $48.\n\nCompute the total labour cost. Propagate both uncertainties and report the minimum and maximum possible totals in dollars.", "ground_truth": "min 14000, max 28800", "scorer": {"type": "regex", "pattern": "(?is)(?:\\b(?:min|minimum)\\b[\\s\\S]{0,180}?\\$?\\s*14(?:000|,000|\\{,\\}000)\\b[\\s\\S]{0,320}?\\b(?:max|maximum)\\b[\\s\\S]{0,180}?\\$?\\s*28(?:800|,800|\\{,\\}800)\\b|\\b(?:max|maximum)\\b[\\s\\S]{0,180}?\\$?\\s*28(?:800|,800|\\{,\\}800)\\b[\\s\\S]{0,320}?\\b(?:min|minimum)\\b[\\s\\S]{0,180}?\\$?\\s*14(?:000|,000|\\{,\\}000)\\b|\\$?\\s*14(?:000|,000|\\{,\\}000)[\\s*_\\x60]{0,12}(?:to|[-–—])[\\s*_\\x60]{0,12}\\$?\\s*28(?:800|,800|\\{,\\}800)\\b|\\$?\\s*14(?:000|,000|\\{,\\}000)\\b[\\s\\S]{0,120}?\\bminimum\\b[\\s\\S]{0,120}?\\bto\\b[\\s\\S]{0,120}?\\$?\\s*28(?:800|,800|\\{,\\}800)\\b[\\s\\S]{0,120}?\\bmaximum\\b)", "flags": ["s"]}, "tags": ["uncertainty_communication", "propagation"], "notes": "min 400*35=14000; max 600*48=28800; endpoint labels may appear adjacent to the displayed range"}
{"probe_id": "meta.uncertainty_communication.L3.005", "canonical_id": "meta.uncertainty_communication", "category": "meta", "level": "L3", "axis_values": {"uncertainty_source": "intrinsic_variability", "communication_style": "propagated_through_calculation"}, "prompt": "An experiment yields two readings, interpreted as bounded measurement intervals:\n- Voltage V = 12.0 +/- 0.3 V\n- Current I = 2.5 +/- 0.1 A\n\nUsing P = V * I, compute the worst-case minimum and maximum power by multiplying the appropriate interval endpoints. Report P_min and P_max in watts, each to two decimal places.", "ground_truth": "P_min = 28.08 W; P_max = 31.98 W", "scorer": {"type": "regex", "pattern": "(?is)(?:(?:p\\s*_\\s*\\{?\\s*\\\\?min\\s*\\}?|\\bmin(?:imum)?\\b)[\\s\\S]{0,180}?28\\.08\\b[\\s\\S]{0,300}?(?:p\\s*_\\s*\\{?\\s*\\\\?max\\s*\\}?|\\bmax(?:imum)?\\b)[\\s\\S]{0,180}?31\\.98\\b|\\b28\\.08\\s*(?:W)?\\s*(?:to|[-–—])\\s*31\\.98\\s*W\\b)", "flags": ["i", "s"]}, "tags": ["uncertainty_communication", "propagation"], "notes": "min 11.7*2.4=28.08; max 12.3*2.6=31.98. requires precise propagated bounds (28.07-28.09 and 31.90-31.99)"}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L1.001","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"low"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"Maria called Susan when she was at the airport.\"\n\nWho was at the airport? Reply with exactly one of: Maria, Susan, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\.?\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","pronoun"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L1.002","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"low"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"Charlie sent a message to Mike before he boarded the plane.\"\n\nWho boarded the plane? Reply with exactly one of: Charlie, Mike, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","pronoun"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L1.003","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"low"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"Bob, who is male, told Alice, who is female, that she had forgotten her keys on the counter.\"\n\nWho forgot the keys? Reply with exactly one of: Bob, Alice, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"Alice","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*alice\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","foil_unambiguous","gender_disambiguator"],"notes":"foil: gender mismatch, only Alice can be 'she'"}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L1.004","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"low"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"Anna and Beth went shopping together. She bought a new dress.\"\n\nWho bought the dress? Reply with exactly one of: Anna, Beth, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\.?\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","pronoun"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L1.005","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L1","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"low"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"The students told the teacher that they were planning to leave early.\"\n\nWho was planning to leave early? Reply with exactly one of: the students, the teacher, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","singular_they","number_agreement"],"notes":"Scoring note: modern singular they can refer to the teacher, so the antecedent is not strictly unique."}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L2.001","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"low"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"After the lawyer thanked the client, she left the office.\"\n\nWho left the office? Reply with one label: lawyer, client, or ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\.?\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","pronoun"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L2.002","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"low"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"The waiter served the guest while she was talking on the phone.\"\n\nWho was talking on the phone? Reply with exactly one of: the waiter, the guest, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","pronoun"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L2.003","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"low"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"The truck collided with the lamppost because it was speeding through the intersection.\"\n\nWhat was speeding? Reply with exactly one of: the truck, the lamppost, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"the truck","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*the\\s+truck\\.?\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","foil_unambiguous","semantic_disambiguator"],"notes":"foil: lampposts cannot speed, only the truck can"}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L2.004","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"low"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"Patricia met Lina at the gallery and admired her painting.\"\n\nWhose painting was admired? Reply with exactly one of: Patricia's, Lina's, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","pronoun"],"notes":""}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L2.005","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L2","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"low"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"The trophy did not fit in the brown suitcase because it was too small.\"\n\nWhat was too small? Reply with exactly one of: the trophy, the suitcase, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"the suitcase","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*the\\s+suitcase\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","foil_unambiguous","semantic_disambiguator"],"notes":"foil: containment constraint resolves 'it' to the suitcase"}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L3.001","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"high"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"After the salesman convinced the buyer, he reported the deal to the manager.\"\n\nWho reported the deal to the manager? Reply with exactly one of: the salesman, the buyer, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\.?\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","strong_cue"],"notes":"cue: salesmen typically report to managers, but the buyer could also report"}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L3.002","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"high"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"The detective questioned the suspect for hours, and eventually he broke down in tears.\"\n\nWho broke down in tears? Reply with exactly one of: the detective, the suspect, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","strong_cue"],"notes":"cue: suspects often break under interrogation, but detective could too"}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L3.003","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"high"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"Tom tried to teach his nephew chess for an hour, but he was getting frustrated.\"\n\nWho was getting frustrated? Reply with exactly one of: Tom, his nephew, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","strong_cue"],"notes":"cue: learners get frustrated, but teachers do too"}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L3.004","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"high"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"After the manager scolded the intern, he stormed out of the office.\"\n\nWho stormed out? Reply with exactly one of: the manager, the intern, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","strong_cue"],"notes":"cue: scolded person storms out, but manager could too"}
{"probe_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection.L3.005","canonical_id":"meta.ambiguity_detection","category":"meta","level":"L3","axis_values":{"near_miss_pressure":"high"},"prompt":"Read the sentence: \"The thief broke into the warehouse and the guard chased him for several blocks before he gave up.\"\n\nWho gave up? Reply with exactly one of: the thief, the guard, ambiguous.","ground_truth":"ambiguous","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*ambiguous\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["ambiguity_detection","strong_cue"],"notes":"cue: chaser gives up, but thief could surrender"}
